l and reciprocity formulas.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-first-mertens-theorem-for-primes","used_for":"Mertens estimate in arithmetic progressions.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-first-orthogonality-relation-for-irreducible-complex-characters","used_for":"Dirichlet-character orthogonality.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-first-supplement-to-quadratic-reciprocity","used_for":"Hilbert reciprocity.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-gregory-leibniz-series-for-pi-from-a-finite-remainder","used_for":"The χ₄ Leibniz-series example.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-irreducible-representations-of-a-finite-abelian-group-over-a-splitting-field-are-one-dimensional","used_for":"Identifying Dirichlet characters with irreducible characters.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-landau-dirichlet-series","used_for":"Boundary nonvanishing arguments for Dirichlet L-functions.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-machine-acceptance-is-ce-complete","used_for":"The computably-enumerable strictness witness.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-machine-acceptance-is-undecidable","used_for":"PCP and CFG undecidability reductions.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-many-one-reductions-transfer-decidability-and-recognizability","used_for":"Transferring undecidability through reductions.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-multiplicative-group-of-a-finite-field-is-cyclic","used_for":"Counting finite-field square classes.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-p-adic-newton-criterion","used_for":"Selmer's cubic and the 2-adic square criterion.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-pumping-lemma-for-context-free-languages","used_for":"CFL separation witness.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-pumping-lemma-for-regular-languages","used_for":"Regular-language separation witness.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-quadratic-reciprocity","used_for":"Hilbert reciprocity.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-riemann-zeta-meromorphic-continuation","used_for":"Principal L-function pole and residue.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-second-column-orthogonality-relation-for-irreducible-complex-characters","used_for":"Dirichlet-character column orthogonality.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-second-supplement-to-quadratic-reciprocity","used_for":"Hilbert reciprocity.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-square-criterion-in-q2","used_for":"The 2-adic Hilbert-symbol formula.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-square-criterion-in-qp-for-odd-p","used_for":"Odd-prime Hilbert-symbol formula.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-three-sat-is-np-complete","used_for":"NP-hardness propagation from 3-SAT.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-weak-approximation-for-rational-places","used_for":"Global square-class approximation and Hasse-Minkowski.","opened":true}],"concerns":[{"id":"thm-proper-inclusions-in-the-chomsky-hierarchy","concern":"The statement says the containments are strict for a fixed finite alphabet Σ. This is false for a unary alphabet: every unary context-free language is regular, while the proof's witness {a^n b^n} is not a language over a fixed unary Σ. The statement needs an alphabet-size hypothesis or a differently scoped hierarchy.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"lem-computation-history-domino-encoding","concern":"The alleged construction only names “standard accepting-tail tiles” and generic local transition tiles. It does not specify their words or establish the alignment invariant that rules out spurious PCP matches; step 3.2 simply asserts that a match decomposes into legal successive configurations. That is the nontrivial content of the PCP reduction.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"thm-cfg-universality-is-undecidable","concern":"Step 1.1 asserts that histories with an illegal adjacent transition form a CFL because a PDA can “guess that window and verify the mismatch,” but gives neither a history encoding with the needed reversal/alignment nor a grammar/PDA that compares arbitrary adjacent configurations. The cited history item supplies decidability, not this CFL-complement construction.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"thm-myhill-isomorphism-theorem-for-creative-sets","concern":"Step 1.2 claims q(y)=s_2^1(r,y,r) is one-one merely because different parameters give different residual machine codes; neither acceptability nor the stated s-m-n theorem makes specialization injective. Also step 3.1 says injectivity of f and g makes its alternating chains distinct, but f∘g can have cycles. Thus the proposed effective Cantor–Bernstein construction is not justified.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"thm-hasse-minkowski-over-the-rationals","concern":"In the dimension-four branch, step 2.1 cites global-square-class approximation for v_p(t)=0 outside S except p_0, but the cited lemma guarantees only even valuation. A global-square rescaling can likely repair this. Also step 3.1 uses that dimension-four t before the later n≥5 branch that defines its own t, making the branch structure unclear.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"thm-three-sat-reduces-to-directed-hamiltonian-cycle","concern":"The converse says a clause detour “can only replace one crossbar edge” in its own variable gadget, but each clause vertex c_j is connected to literal-pair endpoints from every variable occurring in its clause. A path can enter c_j from one occurrence and leave toward another; the asserted local-detour invariant and the preceding claim that only gadget identifications connect variable gadgets are therefore not established.","severity":"would-be-fatal"}],"alerts":[],"seams_checked":[]}